<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Date Munenari</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1818">1818</a>–<a href="/years/1892">1892</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Date Munenari was lord of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uwajima">Uwajima</a> Domain from 1844 to 1858 and one of the 'four wise lords' of the late <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edo-period">Edo period</a>. He promoted Western learning, sheltering the scholar <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/takano-choei">Takano Choei</a> and employing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/omura-masujiro">Murata Zoroku</a>, later Omura Masujiro, and had a steamship built by local craftsmen. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meiji-restoration">Meiji Restoration</a> he served the new government and negotiated a treaty with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qing-empire">Qing China</a> in 1871.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/date-munenari.jpg" alt="Date Munenari" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: AnonymousUnknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Date_Munenari_(coloured).jpeg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
